  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q40-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "40",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p11-q40-definition-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A set of three or more natural numbers is said to be a ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "magical set",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " if each member of the set divides the sum of all the numbers in the set. If the numbers in a magical set have no common factors other than 1, then it is said to be a ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "primitive magical set",
              "marks": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q40-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is a magical set.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1+2+3=6$, and each of $1$, $2$ and $3$ divides $6$.",
          "worked": "$$1+2+3=6.$$ Since $6\\div1=6$, $6\\div2=3$ and $6\\div3=2$, every member divides the sum. Therefore, $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is a magical set.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q40-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that given a magical set, you can always add an additional number to form a new magical set. Hint: Find the sum of the numbers in the set.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Add the sum $S$ of the original set.\n\nThe new sum is $2S$, which is divisible by every original member and by $S$.",
          "worked": "Let $S$ be the sum of the original magical set. Every member divides $S$, so every member also divides $2S$. Since the members are positive and there are at least three of them, $S$ exceeds every member and is an additional number. Add $S$ to the set. The new sum is $S+S=2S$, which is divisible by $S$ and by every original member. Hence the new set is magical.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q40-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove that $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is the unique primitive magical set with three elements.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Every three-element magical set is $\\{a,2a,3a\\}$.\n\nPrimitivity forces $a=1$, giving $\\{1,2,3\\}$.",
          "worked": "Let $\\{a,b,c\\}$ be a primitive magical set, with $a<b<c$. Since $c$ divides $a+b+c$, it divides $a+b$. Also $0<a+b<2c$, so\n$$a+b=c.$$\nSince $b$ divides $a+b+c$, it divides $a+c=2a+b$, and hence divides $2a$. As $0<2a<2b$, we obtain\n$$b=2a.$$\nTherefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}c&=a+b\\\\c&=a+2a\\\\c&=3a.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the set is $\\{a,2a,3a\\}$, whose greatest common factor is $a$. Primitivity gives $a=1$, so the set must be $\\{1,2,3\\}$. Part (a) shows that this set is magical, and its greatest common factor is $1$. Therefore it is the unique primitive magical set with three elements.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 116
      }
    ]
  },
